      "sanskrit": "tatra kāmya-pratiṣiddha-karma-pravṛtti-kāraṇam apanudya bhagavan-matam anuvartituṃ tat-kāraṇāvadhāraṇāya arjuna uvāca atheti | dhyāyato viṣayān puṃsaḥ ity ādinā pūrvam anartha-mūlam uktam | sāmprataṃ ca prakṛter guṇa-saṃmūḍhā ity ādinā bahu-vistaraṃ kathitam | tatra kiṃ sarvāṇy api sama-prādhānyena kāraṇāni | athavaikam eva mukhyaṃ kāraṇam itarāṇi tu tat-sahakārīṇi kevalam | tatrādye sarveṣāṃ pṛthak pṛthaṅ nivāraṇe mahān prayāsaḥ syāt | antye tv ekasminn eva nirākṛte kṛta-kṛtyatā syād ity ato brūhi me kena hetunā prayuktaḥ prerito 'yaṃ tvan-matānanuvartī sarva-jñāna-vimūḍhaḥ puruṣaḥ pāpam anarthānubandhi sarvaṃ phalābhisandhi-puraḥ-saraṃ kāmyaṃ citrādi śatru-vadha-sādhanaṃ ca śyanādi pratiṣiddhaṃ ca kalañja-bhakṣaṇādi bahu-vidhaṃ karmācarati svayaṃ kartum anicchann api na tu nivṛtti-lakṣaṇaṃ parama-puruṣārthānubandhi tvad-upadiṣṭaṃ karmecchann api karoti | na ca pāratantryaṃ vinetthaṃ sambhavati | ato yena balād iva niyojito rājñeva bhṛtyas tvan-mata-viruddhaṃ sarvānarthānubandhitvaṃ jānann api tādṛśaṃ karmācarati tam anartha-mārga-pravartakaṃ māṃ prati brūhi jñātvā samucchedāyety arthaḥ | he vārṣṇeya vṛṣṇi-vaṃśe man-mātāmaha-kule kṛpayāvatīrṇeti sambodhanena vārṣṇeyī-suto 'ham tvayā apekṣaṇīya iti sūcayati",
      "ourRendering": "There, having removed the cause of the initiating of desire-prompted and forbidden karma, in order to go on following the Lord's view, for the ascertainment of that cause, Arjuna said, ‘now’ — the verse's opening word. By ‘one who broods on the objects of sense, of a man’ and so on, the root of misfortune was stated before. Now, by ‘of prakṛti, those bewildered by the guṇas’ and so on, it has been told at great length. There: is it that all of them, even, are causes with equal primacy? Or else: is there one alone the principal cause, the others being merely co-operating with it? In that case — the first alternative — in the warding-off of all of them severally, one by one, great would be the effort. In the last alternative, but, with the one alone being removed, the accomplishing of one's task would follow — so Arjuna thinks. Hence: ‘tell me, by what cause, impelled, driven, is this man, the one who does not follow your view, wholly bewildered of all knowledge — himself not wishing to do it, even — performing karma of many kinds: evil, entailing misfortune, altogether wholly preceded by intent on the fruit, kāmya — such as the Citrā-rite and the rest, the means for slaying an enemy — and forbidden — such as the Śyena-rite and the rest, the eating of kalañja and the rest — but not the karma characterized by cessation, entailing the highest goal of man, taught by you, though wishing it, even — does he do that? For dependence cannot otherwise be possible in this way.’ Hence: ‘the one by whom, as if by force, like — harnessed by a king, as — like a servant, though knowing that it is opposed to your view and entails every misfortune, performs such karma — him, the one who sets me on the path of misfortune, tell it to me, regarding me, having known him, for his complete extirpation’ — that is the sense. By ‘O Vārṣṇeya’ — the vocative — [Arjuna] suggests: ‘having descended by grace into the lineage of Vṛṣṇi, my maternal grandfather's family — I, the son of the Vṛṣṇi-woman, am to be regarded by you.’",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.36",
      "textualNote": "The on-disk witness prints nipekṣaṇīya, which is not a standard formation. The reading given is apekṣaṇīyaḥ (gerundive of apa-√īkṣ, ‘to be regarded, looked to, depended upon’), which fits both the grammar (a gerundive is required after tvayā) and the sense (Arjuna urging that Kṛṣṇa, as kinsman, ought to attend to him) — almost certainly a single-letter scribal slip (n for the expected a).",
