      "sanskrit": "yad uktaṃ rāga-dveṣau vyavasthitāv [gītā 3.34] ity atra śāstra-niṣiddhe 'pīndriyārthe para-strī-sambhāṣaṇādau rāga ity atra pṛcchati atheti | kena prayojaka-kartrānicchann api vidhi-niṣedha-śāstrārtha-jñānavattvāt pāpe pravartitum icchā-rahito 'pi balād iveti prayojaka-preraṇa-vaśāt prayojyasyāpīcchā samyag utpadyate iti bhāvaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "As for what was said — ‘rāga and dveṣa stand fixed’ (Gītā 3.34) — here, in what is forbidden by śāstra, even in an indriya's object — such as conversing with another's wife — there is rāga: here he asks, in the word ‘atha’. By what — by the impelling agent —, though not wishing it, even though possessed of the knowledge of the sense of the injunction and the prohibition of śāstra, to engage in evil — devoid of desire though he is — as if by force? So it is asked. Because of the force of the impeller's driving-on, a desire does properly arise even in the one impelled — that is the point.",
