      "sanskrit": "indriyasya ity ādau śāstra-niṣiddhe 'pi para-dāra-sambhāṣaṇādau rāgo vyavasthita iti yad uktaṃ tatrārjunaḥ pṛcchati atha keneti | he vārṣṇeya vṛṣṇi-vaṃśodbhava | śubhādibhyaś ceti prayuktaḥ preritaḥ pāpaṃ carati niṣedha-śāstrārtha-jñānāt tac-caritam anicchann api balād iveti | prayojakecchāpannatayā prayojye 'pīcchā prajāyate | sa kim īśvaraḥ, pūrva-saṃskāro vā? tatrādyaḥ sākṣitvāt kāruṇikatvāc ca na pāpe prerakaḥ | na ca paro jaḍatvād iti praśnārthaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "In [the verse] beginning with ‘indriyasya’, it was said that rāga is fixed even upon what is forbidden by the śāstra — beginning with conversing with another man's wife. There Arjuna asks, ‘impelled by what’ — O Vārṣṇeya, born in the lineage of Vṛṣṇi — impelled, that is, driven, from things beginning with the good, he practises evil, out of the knowledge of the meaning of the prohibitive scripture: that conduct, not wishing it, even so, as if by force. Because, in the one who is impelled, the impeller's desire comes to be assumed as his own, desire arises even in the one impelled. Is that one the Lord, or a prior saṃskāra? Of these, the first is not the impeller toward evil, because he is the witness and because he is compassionate; nor is the other, because it is insentient — that is the point of the question.",
