      "locus": "3.37",
      "sanskrit": "śrī-bhagavān uvāca kāma iti | yas tvayā pṛṣṭo hetur balād anartha-mārge pravartakaḥ sa eṣa kāma eva mahān śatruḥ | yan-nimittā sarvānartha-prāptiḥ prāṇinām | nanu krodho 'py abhicārādau pravartako dṛṣṭa ity ata āha krodha eṣaḥ | kāma eva kenacid dhetunā pratihataḥ krodhatvena pariṇamate 'taḥ krodho 'py eṣa kāma eva | etasminn eva mahā-vairiṇi nivārite sarva-puruṣārtha-prāptir ity arthaḥ | tan-nivāraṇopāya-jñānāya tat-kāraṇam āha rajo-guṇa-samudbhavaḥ | duḥkha-pravṛtti-balātmako rajo-guṇa eva samudbhavaḥ kāraṇaṃ yasya | ataḥ kāraṇānuvidhāyitvāt kāryasya so 'pi tathā | yadyapi tamo-guṇo 'pi tasya kāraṇaṃ tathāpi duḥkhe pravṛttau ca rajasa eva prādhānyāt tasyaiva nirdeśaḥ | etena sāttvikyā vṛttyā rajasi kṣīṇe so 'pi kṣīyata ity uktam | athavā tasya katham anartha-mārge pravartakatvam ity ata āha rajo-guṇasya pravṛttyādi-lakṣaṇasya samudbhavo yasmāt | kāmo hi viṣayābhilāṣātmakaḥ svayam udbhūto rajaḥ pravartayan puruṣaṃ duḥkhātmake karmaṇi pravartayati | tenāyam avaśyaṃ hantavya ity abhiprāyaḥ | nanu sāma-dāna-bheda-daṇḍāś catvāra upāyās tatra prathama-trikasyāsambhave caturtho daṇḍaḥ prayoktavyo na tu haṭhād evety āśaṅkya trayāṇām asambhavaṃ vaktuṃ viśinaṣṭi mahāśano mahā-pāpmeti | mahad aśanam asyeti mahāśanaḥ | yat pṛthivyāṃ vrīhi-yavaṃ hiraṇyaṃ paśavaḥ striyaḥ | nālam ekasya tat sarvam iti matvā śamaṃ vrajet || iti smṛteḥ | ato na dānena sandhātuṃ śakyaḥ | nāpi sāma-bhedābhyāṃ yato mahā-pāpmātyugraḥ | tena hi balāt prerito 'niṣṭa-phalam api jānan pāpaṃ karoti | ato viddhi jānīhi enaṃ kāmam iha saṃsāre vairiṇam",
      "ourRendering": "The Blessed Lord said, ‘kāma’ — the one that, by you, was asked about as the cause impelling by force on the path of misfortune: that, this, is kāma indeed, the great enemy. On account of which the arrival of every misfortune befalls living beings. Now: anger too, in black magic and the like, is seen to be an impeller — so it might seem. Hence he states: ‘krodha’ — this kāma itself, blocked by something or other, by some cause, turns into the state of being anger. Hence anger too is this same very kāma itself. In this very great enemy being warded off, the attainment of every human goal follows — thus is the sense. For the sake of knowing the means of warding it off, he states its cause: ‘arisen from the guṇa rajas’ — having for its essence the force that impels towards sorrow, the guṇa rajas itself is the arising, the cause, of which it is the effect. Hence, because the effect conforms to its cause, it too is likewise. Although the guṇa tamas is also a cause of it, nevertheless, because rajas alone has the primacy in sorrow and in impelling to action, its designation alone follows. By this it is implied: when rajas is diminished by a sāttvika disposition, it too is diminished. Or else: how is rajas the impeller on the path of misfortune? — hence he states: the arising is of the guṇa rajas characterized by impelling and the like, since kāma, whose nature is longing for the objects of sense, having itself arisen, setting rajas in motion, impels the man into karma whose nature is sorrow. By this, necessarily, it is to be slain — such is the purport. Now: conciliation, gift, division, and force are the four expedients; among them, if the first triad is impossible, the fourth, force, should be employed — not out of sheer violence merely. Anticipating this, in order to state the impossibility of the three former means, he specifies: ‘the great devourer’, ‘the great evildoer’ — great is the eating of it: hence ‘the great devourer’. ‘Whatever is on earth — rice and barley, gold, cattle, women — is not enough for one man, the whole of it’ — reckoning so, let him go to contentment: so runs a smṛti verse. Hence it cannot be brought to terms by a gift; nor even by conciliation and division, since the great evildoer is exceedingly fierce. For, driven on by force by it, one does evil even while knowing the result to be undesired. Therefore: know, understand this kāma here in saṃsāra to be the enemy.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.37",
      "excerpt": true,
      "excerptNote": "Two blocks are left out. (1) The opening, from ‘evam arjunena pṛṣṭe’ through the citation ‘ātmaivedam agra āsīt … atha karma kurvīya’ (a Bṛhadāraṇyaka-style pūrvapakṣa on kāma as the root of all willing), is not shown; the entry opens directly at Kṛṣṇa's own reply, ‘śrī-bhagavān uvāca kāma iti’. (2) The closing block, from ‘tad etat sarvaṃ vivṛtaṃ vārtika-kāraiḥ’ to the end, in which Madhusūdana quotes six ślokas of Sureśvara's Vārtika (on the same Bṛhadāraṇyaka passage) plus a citation from Manu reinforcing that kāma alone — not fate, not another agent — impels unwanted action, is not shown. What remains is the continuous core argument: kāma identified as the enemy, kāma and krodha identified as one, the two readings of rajo-guṇa-samudbhavaḥ, and mahāśana/mahā-pāpmā explained through the four political expedients and a smṛti citation.",
