      "locus": "3.37",
      "sanskrit": "eṣa kāma eva viṣayābhilāṣātmakaḥ puruṣaṃ pāpe pravartayati tenaiva prayuktaḥ puruṣaḥ pāpaṃ caratīty arthaḥ | eṣa kāma eva pṛthaktvena dṛśyamāna eṣa pratyakṣaḥ krodho bhavati | kāma eva kenacit pratihato bhūtvā krodhākāreṇa pariṇamatīty arthaḥ | kāmo rajo-guṇa-samudbhava iti rājasāt kāmād eva tāmasaḥ krodho jāyate ity arthaḥ | kāmasya apekṣita-pūraṇena nivṛttiḥ syād iti cen nety āha mahāśano mahad aśanaṃ yasya saḥ",
      "ourRendering": "This very kāma, whose nature is craving for objects, sets the man going into evil; impelled by that very thing, the man engages in evil — that is the sense. This same kāma, seen separately, itself becomes visible anger. kāma itself, having been thwarted by something, turns into the form of anger — that is the sense. ‘kāma is arisen from the guṇa rajas’ — thus (the verse says): from the rājasic kāma alone is the tāmasic anger born — that is the sense. Of kāma, through the filling of what is sought, might cessation occur? — if it be objected — no: to this he states ‘the great devourer’ — ‘whose food is great’.",
