      "work": "Gūḍhārtha-dīpikā",
      "locus": "3.38",
      "sanskrit": "tasya mahā-pāpmatvena vairtvam eva dṛṣṭāntaiḥ spaṣṭayati dhūmeneti | tatra śarīrārambhāt prāg-antaḥ-karaṇa-sthālabdha-vṛttikatvāt sūkṣmaḥ kāmaḥ śarīrārambhakeṇa karmaṇā sthūla-śarīrāvacchinne labdha-vṛttike 'ntaḥkaraṇe kṛtābhivyaktiḥ san sthūlo bhavati | sa eva viṣayasya cintyamānatāvasthāyāṃ punaḥ punar udricyamānaḥ sthūlataro bhavati | sa eva punar viṣayasya bhujyamānatāvasthāyām atyantodrekaṃ prāptaḥ sthūlatamo bhavati | tatra prathamāvasthāyāṃ dṛṣṭāntaḥ -- yathā dhūmena sahajenāprakāśātmakena prakāśātmako vahnir āvriyate | dvitīyāvasthāyāṃ dṛṣṭāntaḥ - yathādarśo malenāsahajenādarśotpatty-anantaram udriktena | ca-kāro 'vāntara-vaidharmya-sūcanārtha āvriyata iti kriyānukarṣaṇārthaś ca | tṛtīyāvasthāyāṃ dṛṣṭāntaḥ - yatholbena jarāyuṇā garbha-veṣṭana-carmaṇātisthūlena sarvato nirudhyāvṛtas tathā prakāra-trayeṇāpi tena kāmenedam āvṛtam | atra dhūmenāvṛto 'pi vahnir dāhādi-lakṣaṇaṃ sva-kāryaṃ karoti | malenāvṛtas tv ādarśaḥ pratibimba-grahaṇa-lakṣaṇaṃ svakāryaṃ na karoti | svacchatā-dharma-mātra-tirodhānāt svarūpatas tūpalabhyata eva | ulbenāvṛtas tu garbho na hasta-pādādi-prasāraṇa-rūpaṃ sva-kāryaṃ karoti na vā svarūpata upalabhyata iti viśeṣaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "That it is the enemy, by its being the great evildoer — this he now makes clear by illustrations: ‘by smoke’ — thus. There: before the body's beginning, because of not yet having obtained a function while residing in the antaḥkaraṇa, kāma is subtle; delimited by the gross body, having obtained its function, in the antaḥkaraṇa, having its manifestation made, by that which initiates the body, the karma, it becomes gross. That very same kāma — in the state of the sense-object being dwelt upon in thought — growing again and again, becomes grosser. That very same kāma again — in the state of the sense-object being enjoyed — having reached an excess without limit, becomes grossest. There, in the first state, the illustration is: just as fire, whose nature is luminous, is covered by smoke, innate, whose nature is non-luminous. In the second state, the illustration is: just as a mirror is covered by dirt, not innate, accumulated immediately after the mirror's coming into being. The word ‘and’ serves to indicate a subordinate distinction — thus — and to carry the verb over as well. In the third state, the illustration is: just as, by the caul, the placental membrane, the skin enveloping the embryo, exceedingly thick, on all sides, confined, [the embryo] is covered — so too, by all three modes, even, this [knowledge], by that kāma, by it, is covered. Here: fire, covered by smoke, even so, still performs its own function characterized by burning and the like. The mirror, however, covered by dirt, does not perform its own function characterized by the taking-up of a reflection. Because of the mere concealment of the quality of transparency, in respect of its own form, still, it is perceived indeed. The embryo, however, covered by the caul, neither performs its own function whose form is the stretching-out of hand, foot, and the like, nor is it perceived in respect of its own form: so runs the distinction.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.38",
