      "locus": "3.38",
      "sanskrit": "mṛdu-madhya-tīvra-bhāvena trividhasya kāmasya dhūma-malolbaneti krameṇa dṛṣṭāntān āha dhūmenet | yathā dhūmenāvṛto 'nujjvalo 'pi vahnir auṣṇādikaṃ kiṃcit karoti malenāvṛto darpaṇaḥ svacchatā-tirodhānāt pratibimbaṃ na śaknoti grahītum ulbena jarā-guṇāvṛto garbhas tu pādādi-prasārarṃ na śaknoti kartuṃ na copalabhyate | tathā mṛdunā kāmenāvṛtaṃ jñānaṃ kathaṃcit tattvārthaṃ grahītuṃ śaknoti madhyenāvṛtaṃ na śaknoti | tīvreṇāvṛtaṃ tu prasartum api na śaknoti, na ca pratīyata ity arthaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "By reason of the mild, middling and intense states, for the threefold kāma, in due order — by the triad ‘smoke, dirt, membrane’ — he states the illustrations, in the word ‘dhūmena’. Just as fire, covered by smoke, though not blazing, does something slight — heat and the like; a mirror covered by dirt, because its clarity is concealed, is not able to grasp a reflection; but an embryo, covered by the ulba — of the nature of the caul — is not able to make even the stretching of a foot and so on, nor is it perceived at all — so too: knowledge covered by mild kāma is able to grasp the sense of a thing's reality somehow; covered by the middling kāma it is not able; but covered by the intense kāma it is not even able to proceed at all, nor is it apprehended at all: that is the sense.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.38",
      "textualNote": "The witness prints ‘dhūma-malolbaneti’ for the expected dhūma-mala-ulbena iti (ulbena + iti regularly sandhis to ulbeneti; the witness's medial vowel reads a where e is expected — a one-letter slip, read here as the dvandva dhūma-mala-ulbena in the instrumental, citing the verse's own trio dhūmena…malena…ulbena). Separately, ‘āha dhūmenet’ is read as āha dhūmena iti (dhūmena + iti regularly sandhis to dhūmeneti; the witness's final -i is missing). And ‘pādādi-prasārarṃ’ is read as pādādi-prasāram (an extra -r- in the witness). None of these affect the sense; all three are given as printed in `sanskrit` above.",
