      "locus": "3.39",
      "sanskrit": "tathā tenedam āvṛtam iti saṅgraha-vākyaṃ vivṛṇoty āvṛtam iti | jñāyate 'neneti jñānam antaḥkaraṇaṃ viveka-vijñānaṃ veda-śabda-nirdiṣṭam etena kāmenāvṛtam | tathāpy āpātataḥ sukha-hetutvād upādeyaḥ syād ity ata āha jñānino nitya-vairiṇā | ajño hi viṣaya-bhoga-kāle kāmaṃ mitram iva paśyaṃs tat-kārye duḥkhe prāpte vairitvaṃ jānāti kāmenāhaṃ duḥkhitvam āpādita iti | jñānī tu bhoga-kāle 'pi jānāty anenāham anarthe praveśita iti | ato vivekī duḥkhī bhavati bhoga-kāle ca tat-pariṇāme cāneneti jñānino 'sau nitya-vairīti sarvathā tena hantavya evety arthaḥ | tarhi kiṃ svarūpo 'sāv ity ata āha kāma-rūpeṇa | kāma icchā tṛṣṇā saiva rūpaṃ yasya tena | he kaunteyeti sambandhāviṣkāreṇa premāṇaṃ sūcayati | nanu vivekino hantavyo 'py avivekina upādeyaḥ syād ity ata āha duṣpūreṇānalena ca | ca-kāra upamārthaḥ | na vidyate 'laṃ paryāptir yasyety analo vahniḥ | sa yathā haviṣā pūrayitum aśakyas tathāyam api bhogenety arthaḥ | ato nirantaraṃ santāpa-hetutvād vivekina ivāvivekino 'pi heya evāsau | tathā ca smṛtiḥ - na jātu kāmaḥ kāmānām upabhogena śāmyati | haviṣā kṛṣṇa-vartmeva bhūya evābhivardhate || iti | athavecchāyā viṣaya-siddhi-nivartyatvād icchā-rūpaḥ kāmo viṣaya-bhogena svayam eva nivartiṣyate kiṃ tatrāti-nirbandhenety ata uktaṃ duṣpūreṇānalena ceti | viṣaya-siddhyā tat-kālam icchātirodhāne 'pi punaḥ prādurbhāvān na viṣaya-siddhir icchā-nivartikā | kintu viṣaya-doṣa-dṛṣṭir evatatheti bhāvaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "Thus, he explains the summary statement, ‘by this this is covered’ — ‘is covered’ — is known by this — ‘jñāna’: the antaḥkaraṇa, discriminative knowledge, pointed to by a word of the Veda. By this, by kāma, it is covered. Nevertheless, because of being a cause of pleasure at first glance, might it be thought desirable — so it might be objected — hence he states: ‘of the knower’, ‘by the perpetual enemy’. The ignorant one, indeed, regarding kāma as a friend, at the time of enjoying the sense-object, once sorrow, its effect, has arisen, knows the state of being an enemy: ‘by kāma I have been reduced to the state of being miserable.’ The knower, but, even at the time of enjoyment, knows: ‘by this I have been thrust into misfortune.’ Hence the discriminating one is sorrowful both at the time of enjoyment and in its outcome, by this — of the knower this one is the perpetual enemy: in every way is it to be slain by him — that is the sense. Then: what is its own-form, this one? — so it might be asked — hence he states: ‘by the form of kāma’. Kāma — that itself — wanting, tṛṣṇā, is the form of which, by that — O ‘son of Kuntī’ —: by the disclosure of the relationship, he indicates affection. Now: for the discriminating one is it to be slain, although for the undiscriminating one might it be taken up — so it might be asked — hence he states: ‘by the hard-to-fill one’, ‘by fire’, and. The word ‘and’ has the sense of comparison: not is found sufficiency, fullness, of which — thus — ‘fire’: fire it is. Just as with oblation to fill it up it is unable, so this kāma too, by enjoyment — that is the sense. Hence, because of being a cause of torment incessantly, for the discriminating one as for the undiscriminating one too, it is to be rejected indeed, this one. And so runs a smṛti verse: ‘Not ever does kāma, by the enjoyment of desires, subside — with oblation, like fire, further indeed it grows’ — thus. Or else: of wanting, because it is removable by the accomplishment of its object, kāma, whose form is wanting, by the enjoyment of the sense-object, of itself, just, will cease — what need is there for such excessive insistence in that? Hence it is thereby stated: ‘by the hard-to-fill one’, ‘by fire’, and. By the accomplishment of the sense-object, in the concealment of the wanting for that moment, even though, again, because of its reappearing, the accomplishment of the sense-object is not what removes the wanting; rather, the perceiving of the fault in the sense-object alone is so — such is the purport.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.39",
      "textualNote": "The on-disk witness prints svarūo, which is not a standard formation. The reading given is svarūpaḥ (‘of what own-form’), restoring a single dropped akṣara (pa) that the question ‘tarhi kiṃ svarūpo 'sāv’ (‘then, what is its own form?’) grammatically requires and that the reply — kāma icchā tṛṣṇā saiva rūpaṃ yasya — directly answers.",
