      "locus": "3.39",
      "sanskrit": "kāma eva hi jīvasyāvidyety āha āvṛtam iti | nitya-vairiṇy ato 'sau sarva-prakāreṇa hantavya iti bhāvaḥ | kāma-rūpeṇa kāmākāreṇājñānenety arthaḥ | ca-kāra ivārthe | analo yathā haviṣā pūrayitum aśakyas tathā kāmo 'pi bhogenety arthaḥ | yad uktam - na jātu kāmaḥ kāmānām upabhogena śāṃyati | haviṣā kṛṣṇa-vartmeva bhūya evābhivardhate || [bhp 9.19.14] iti",
      "ourRendering": "kāma alone, indeed, is the jīva's avidyā — thus he states, in the word ‘āvṛtam’. The perpetual enemy — therefore that one is to be slain in every way — that is the point. ‘By the form of kāma’ — by the shape of kāma, by ignorance — that is the sense. The word ‘ca’ is used in the sense of ‘like’. Just as fire cannot be filled by oblations, so too kāma, even by enjoyment — that is the sense. As it has been said — ‘Never at all does kāma, by the enjoyment of the things desired, subside; with oblations, like the black-pathed one, all the more does it grow’ (Bhāgavata-Purāṇa 9.19.14) — thus.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.39",
      "textualNote": "The on-disk witness prints haviṣā pūrayitum acakyas tathā, where acakyas is not a Sanskrit word; the sense (and the parallel duṣpūreṇa passages elsewhere in this reading) requires aśakyaḥ, ‘unable’ (a + śakya), which sandhis to aśakyas before tathā exactly as acakyas is printed. The reading given is aśakyas.",
