      "locus": "3.39",
      "sanskrit": "uktam arthaṃ sphuṭayati āvṛtam iti | anena kāma-rūpeṇa nitya-vairiṇā jñānino jīvasya jñānam āvṛtam iti sambandhaḥ | ajñasya viṣaya-bhoga-samaye sukhatvāt suhṛd api kāmas tat-kārye duḥkhe sati vairḥ syād vijñasya tu tat-samaye 'pi duḥkhānusandhānād duḥkha-hetur eveti nitya-vairiṇety uktiḥ | tasmāt sarvathā hantavya iti bhāvaḥ | kiṃ ca duṣpūreṇeti | ca-śabda ivārthaḥ | tatrānalo yathā haviṣā pūrayitum aśakyas tathā bhogena kāma ity arthaḥ | smṛtiś caivam āha- na jātu kāmaḥ kāmānām upabhogena śāṃyati | haviṣā kṛṣṇa-vartmeva bhūya evābhivardhate | iti | tasmāt sarveṣāṃ sa nitya-vairīti",
      "ourRendering": "He clarifies the stated point, in the word ‘āvṛtam’. By this ever-hostile one, having the form of kāma, the knowledge of the knowing jīva is covered: such is the connection. For the ignorant one, because it is pleasurable at the time of the enjoyment of a sense-object, kāma, though a friend, would become an enemy when sorrow occurs as its effect; but for the discerning one, since — even at that very time — there is the anticipation of sorrow, it is indeed the cause of sorrow: hence the statement ‘ever-hostile’. Therefore it must be slain in every way: that is the point. Moreover, in the word ‘insatiable’ — here the word ‘ca’ has the force of ‘even’: just as fire there is unable to be filled by an oblation, so too kāma is unable to be filled by enjoyment: that is the sense. And smṛti states likewise: ‘Never indeed is kāma appeased by the enjoyment of what is desired; like fire fed with oblation, it only grows the more’ — thus. Therefore it is the ever-enemy of all: so it says.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.39",
      "textualNote": "The witness prints ‘vairḥ’, which does not parse; read as vairī (nom. sg. masc., ‘an enemy’), the predicate the sentence needs (kāmas … vairī syāt, ‘kāma would become an enemy’) — a one-vowel slip in the source. Baladeva himself names no source for the citation, only ‘smṛtiḥ’; the witness carries a modern editorial tag after it, ‘[bhp 9.19.14]’ — identifying it as Bhāgavata Purāṇa 9.19.14 (Yayāti's words) — which is apparatus, not Baladeva's text, and is dropped here exactly as the trailing ||NN|| verse-end markers are dropped elsewhere. kṛṣṇa-vartman, ‘the black-pathed one’, is the standard kenning for fire.",
