      "sanskrit": "jñāte hi śatror adhiṣṭhāne sukhena sa jetuṃ śakyata iti tad-adhiṣṭhānam āha indriyāṇīti | indriyāṇi śabda-sparśa-rūpa-rasa-gandha-grāhakāṇi śrotrādīni vacanādāna-gamana-visargānanda-janakāni vāg-ādīni ca | manaḥ saṅkalpātmakaṃ buddhir adhyavasāyātmikā ca | asya kāmasyādhiṣṭhānam āśraya ucyate | yata etair indriyādibhiḥ sva-sva-vyāpāravadbhir āśrayair vimohayati vividhaṃ mohayati eṣa kāmo jñānam viveka-jñānam āvṛtyācchādya dehinaṃ dehābhimāninam",
      "ourRendering": "For, its stronghold once known, the enemy can easily be conquered — thus he states its stronghold: ‘the indriyas’ — thus. The indriyas — the graspers of sound, touch, form, taste, and smell: hearing and the rest; and the producers of speech, grasping, going, excretion, and bliss: speech and the rest. Manas, whose nature is intending; and buddhi, whose nature is determining. The stronghold — the support — of this, of kāma, is what is said. Since, by these — the indriyas and the rest, possessed of their own respective functions — as supports, this kāma bewilders in various ways, deludes, veiling — covering — knowledge, discriminative knowledge, the embodied one who identifies himself with the body.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.40",
