      "locus": "3.40",
      "sanskrit": "vairiṇaḥ kāmasya durgeṣu nirjiteṣu tasya jayaḥ sukara iti tāny āha indriyāṇīti | viṣaya-śravaṇādinā saṅkalpenādhyavasāyena ca kāmasyābhivyakteḥ śrotrādīni ca manaś ca buddhiś ca tasyādhiṣṭhānaṃ mahā-durga-rājadhānī-rūpaṃ bhavati viṣayās tu tasya tasya janapadā bodhyāḥ | etair viṣaya-saṃcāribhir indriyādibhir dehinaṃ prakṛti-sṛṣṭa-dehavantaṃ jīvam ātma-jñānodyatam eṣa kāmo vimohayati ātma-jñāna-vimukhaṃ viṣaya-rasa-pravaṇaṃ ca karotīty arthaḥ",
      "ourRendering": "When the strongholds of the enemy, kāma, are conquered, its conquest becomes easy — he states them, in the word ‘indriyāṇi’. Because of the manifestation of kāma through the hearing and the rest of a sense-object, through intention, and through resolve, hearing and the rest, and manas, and buddhi, become its seat, of the form of a great fortress-capital; but the sense-objects are to be understood as its various districts. By these — the indriyas and the rest, which range over the sense-objects — this kāma casts into delusion the embodied one, the jīva possessing a body created by prakṛti, intent upon the knowledge of the self: that is, it makes him one who turns away from the knowledge of the self, and one inclined toward the rasa of the sense-objects — that is the sense.",
