      "locus": "3.42",
      "devanagari": "शत्रुहनने आयुधरूपं ज्ञानं वक्तुं ज्ञेयमाह – इन्द्रियाणीति। ‘असङ्गज्ञानासिमादाय तरातिपारम्’ इति ह्युक्तम्। शरीरादिन्द्रियाणि पराणि उत्कृष्टानि। न केवलं बुद्धेः परः। श्रुत्युक्तप्रकारेणाव्यक्तादपि। ‘अव्यक्तात् पुरुषः परः’ इति श्रुतिः।",
      "sanskrit": "śatru-hanane āyudha-rūpaṃ jñānaṃ vaktuṃ jñeyam āha — indriyāṇīti | 'asaṅga-jñānāsim ādāya tarāti-pāram' iti hy uktam | śarīrād indriyāṇi parāṇi utkṛṣṭāni | na kevalaṃ buddheḥ paraḥ | śruty-ukta-prakāreṇāvyaktād api | 'avyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ' iti śrutiḥ |",
      "ourRendering": "In order to state the knowledge that is the weapon for the killing of the enemy, he states what is to be known — in the words ‘indriyāṇi’. ‘Having taken up the sword of the knowledge that clings to nothing, cross to the far shore beyond’ — so it runs, for it has been said. The indriyas are higher than the body, pre-eminent. Not only beyond buddhi: in the manner śruti states, even beyond the unmanifest. ‘Beyond the unmanifest is puruṣa, higher’ — so says śruti.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/madhva_gita_bhasya.txt, adhyāya 3, ad 42",
      "excerpt": true,
      "excerptNote": "The comment continues past this point with an excursus on guṇopasaṃhāra — that liberation does not follow from knowing one aspect of the Lord stated in one place, but requires gathering the attributes stated everywhere — supported by a Gāruḍa citation, and closes by ruling out the reading that takes the verse's ‘he’ as the jīva. That remainder is not shown here.",
      "textualNote": "The witness prints the opening with irregular spacing (शत्रु हनन आयुधरूपं); it is given here as śatru-hanane āyudha-rūpaṃ. The second citation is Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.3.11.",
