      "locus": "3.42",
      "sanskrit": "yatra citta-praṇidhānena indriyāṇi niyantuṃ śakyante, tad ātma-svarūpaṃ dehādibhyo vivicya darśayati indriyāṇīti | indriyāṇi dehādibhyo grāhyebhyaḥ parāṇi śreṣṭhāny āhuḥ sūkṣmatvāt prakāśakatvāc ca | ataeva tad-vyatiriktatvam apy arthād uktaṃ bhavati | indriyebhyaś ca saṅkalpātmakaṃ manaḥ param tat-pravartakatvāt | manasas tu niścayātmikā buddhiḥ parā | niścaya-pūrvakatvāt saṅkalpasya | yas tu buddheḥ paratas tat-sākṣitvenāvasthitaḥ sarvāntaraḥ sa ātmā | taṃ vimohayati dehinam iti dehi-śabdokta ātmā sa iti parāmṛśyate",
      "ourRendering": "Wherein, by concentration of citta, the indriyas can be controlled — that true nature of the self, distinguished from the body and the rest, he shows, by ‘indriyāṇi…’. The indriyas, than the body and the rest — the graspable things — are said to be higher, more excellent, because of subtlety and because of being illuminative. For this very reason its being distinct from that, even, by implication, stated comes to be. And than the indriyas, manas, whose nature is intention, is higher, because it is what sets them going. But than manas, buddhi, whose nature is determination, is higher. Because determination precedes intention. But that which, beyond buddhi, stands as its witness, the innermost of all — that is the self. ‘Deludes’ him — ‘the embodied one’ thus — is understood: the self, denoted by the word ‘dehin’, is he thus.",
