      "sanskrit": "nanu yathā kathaṃcid bāhyendriya-niyama-sambhave 'py āntara-tṛṣṇā-tyāgo 'tiduṣkara iti cen, na | raso 'py asya paraṃ dṛṣṭvā nivartate ity atra para-darśanasya rasābhidhānīyaka-tṛṣṇā-tyāga-sādhanasya prāg-ukteḥ | tarhi ko 'sau paro yad-darśanāt tṛṣṇā-nivṛttir ity āśaṅkya śuddham ātmānaṃ para-śabda-vācyaṃ dehādibhyo vivicya darśayati indriyāṇīti | śrotrādīni jñānendriyāṇi pañca sthūlaṃ jaḍaṃ paricchinnaṃ bāhyaṃ ca deham apekṣya parāṇi sūkṣmatvāt prakāśakatvād vyāpakatvād antaḥsthatvāc ca prakṛṣṭāny āhuḥ paṇḍitāḥ śrutayo vā | tathendriyebhyaḥ paraṃ manaḥ saṅkalpa-vikalpātmakaṃ tat-pravartakatvāt | tathā manasas tu parā buddhir adhyavasāyātmikā | adhyavasāyo hi niścayas tat-pūrvaka eva saṅkalpādir mano-dharmaḥ | yas tu buddheḥ paratas tad-bhāsakatvenāvasthito yaṃ dehinam indriyādibhir āśrayair yuktaḥ kāmo jñānāvaraṇa-dvāreṇa mohayatīty uktaṃ sa buddher draṣṭā para ātmā | sa eṣa iha praviṣṭaḥ itivad dvyavahitasyāpi dehinas tadā parāmarśaḥ | atrārthe śrutiḥ - indriyebhyaḥ parā hy arthā arthebhyaś ca paraṃ manaḥ | manasas tu parā buddhir buddher ātmā mahān paraḥ || mahataḥ param avyaktam avyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ | puruṣān na paraṃ kiṃcit sā kāṣṭhā sā parā gatiḥ || iti",
      "ourRendering": "Now: somehow, even granting the possibility of restraining the outer indriyas, the abandonment of inner thirst is exceedingly difficult — so it might be objected — if so, no. ‘Relish too, the higher of this one having seen, it ceases’ — thus: here, of the prior statement of the seeing of the higher, the means for abandoning the thirst called ‘rasa’. Then: who is that, the higher, from the seeing of which the cessation of thirst follows? — so it might be asked — anticipating this, he shows, having distinguished the pure self, denoted by the word ‘para’, from the body and the rest: ‘the indriyas’ — thus. Hearing and the rest, the powers of knowledge, five: taking as the point of reference the gross, the inert, the bounded, the external body, higher — because of subtlety, because of being illuminative, because of being pervasive, and because of being situated within — pre-eminent they call them, the learned, or the śruti-texts. So too, higher than the indriyas, is manas, whose nature is proposing and doubting, because of being what sets that in motion. So too, but, than manas higher is buddhi, whose nature is determining. For determination is ascertainment, only preceded by that, saṅkalpa and the rest being a property of manas. He who, but, beyond buddhi, by virtue of illumining that, is situated — whom, the embodied one, joined with the indriyas and the rest as supports, kāma by way of the veiling of knowledge deludes — thus has been said — he, the seer of buddhi, is the supreme self. He, this one, here, has entered — as in that formula —: then, though separated by two even, of the embodied one is the reference. On this point, runs the śruti: ‘Than the indriyas higher, indeed, are the objects; and than the objects higher is manas; than manas, but, higher is buddhi; than buddhi the self, the great, is higher. Higher than the great self is the unmanifest; than the unmanifest higher is puruṣa; not higher than anything is puruṣa — that is the ultimate limit, that is the highest goal’ — thus.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.42",
      "excerpt": true,
      "excerptNote": "The comment continues past the Kaṭha Upaniṣad citation (1.3.10–11) given here with a further stretch — from ‘atrātmanaḥ paratvasyaiva’ to the end — that reconciles the Gītā's word order with the Upaniṣad's, quotes the Vāyu-Purāṇa on the ‘great self’ (mahān ātmā) as the cosmic buddhi, and identifies the avyakta with māyā/prakṛti via a further śruti. That closing scholastic elaboration is not shown here; what is given is the core argument — the ladder of indriyas < manas < buddhi < ātman built directly from the Kaṭha Upaniṣad's own ladder — down to its proof-text.",
      "textualNote": "The on-disk witness prints mahataḥ parama-vyaktam avyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ. The reading given is mahataḥ param avyaktam avyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ, matching the well-attested text of Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.3.11 (param avyaktam, ‘beyond [the mahat] is the unmanifest’) that Madhusūdana is quoting; the witness's parama-vyaktam looks like a compression of param avyaktam with the second a dropped.",
