      "sanskrit": "nanu mudrita-yantrāmbu-nyāyena niṣkāma-karma-pravaṇatayendriya-niyamane kāma-kṣatir iti tvayā pradarśitam | atha daihika-karma-kāle mukta-yantrāmbu-nyāyenendriya-vṛtti-prasāre kāmasya punar ujjīvatāpattiḥ syād iti tatra raso 'py asya paraṃ dṛṣṭvā iti pūrvopadiṣṭena viviktātmānubhavena niḥśeṣā tasya kṣatiḥ syād iti darśayati indriyāṇīti dvābhyām | pāñcabhautikād dehād indriyāṇi parāṇy āhur paṇḍitāḥ | tac cālīkatvāt tato 'tisūkṣmatvāt tad-vināśe 'vināśāc ca | indriyebhyaḥ manaḥ paraṃ jāgare teṣāṃ pravartakatvāt svapne teṣu svasmin vilīneṣu rājya-kartṛtvena sthitatvāc ca | manasas tu buddhiḥ parā, niścayātmaka-buddhi-vṛttyaiva saṅkalpātmaka-mano-vṛtteḥ prasarāt | yas tu buddher api parato 'sti, sa dehī jīvātmā cit-svarūpo dehādi-buddhy-antar-viviktayānubhūtaḥ san niḥśeṣa-kāma-kṣati-hetur bhavatīti | kaṭhāś caivaṃ paṭhanti - indriyebhyaḥ parā hy arthā arthebhyaś ca paraṃ manaḥ | manasas tu parā buddhir buddher ātmā mahān paraḥ | ity ādi | asyārthaḥ - indriyebhyo 'rthā viṣayās tad-ākarsiktatvāt parāḥ pradhāna-bhūtāḥ | viṣayendriya-vyavahārasya mano-mūlatvād arthebhyo manaḥ paraṃ viṣaya-bhogasya niścaya-pūrvakatvāt saṃśayātmakān manaso manaḥ paraṃ viṣaya-bhogasya niścaya-pūrvakatvāt saṃśayātmakān manaso niścayātmikā buddhiḥ parā buddher bhogopakaraṇatvāt tasyāḥ sakāśād bhoktātmā jīvaḥ paraḥ sa cātmā mahān dehendriyāntaḥkaraṇa-svāmīti daihikaṃ karma tu pūrvābhyāsa-vaśāc cakra-bhramitvat setsyati",
      "ourRendering": "But: by the principle of water held by a sealed sluice-mechanism, through inclining toward niṣkāma-karma, in the reining-in of the indriyas, the injury of kāma was shown by you. Now, at the time of bodily karma, by the principle of water released through an opened sluice-mechanism, in the spreading-out of the activity of the indriyas, there could be a recurrence of the revival of kāma — in reply to that, by means of the previously-taught direct experience of the discerned self — taught in the words ‘even the rasa of it ceases, having seen the Supreme’ — he shows that its destruction would be complete, without remainder: in the two verses beginning with ‘indriyāṇi’. The learned call the indriyas higher than the body made of the five elements — because that body is unreal, because the indriyas are extremely subtle relative to it, and because, when that body perishes, they do not perish. manas is higher than the indriyas, because, in the waking state, it is their instigator; and because, in dream, when they have dissolved into it, it stands as the sole sovereign. But buddhi is higher than manas, because it is only through the modification of buddhi — whose nature is ascertainment — that the modification of manas — whose nature is intention — extends into action. But he who exists even beyond buddhi — he is the embodied one, the jīva-self, whose very nature is consciousness, being experienced through an experience discerned from the body and the rest up to and including buddhi — becomes the cause of the complete destruction of kāma. And the Kaṭha reciters recite thus: ‘Higher than the indriyas, indeed, are the objects; and higher than the objects is manas; but higher than manas is buddhi; higher than buddhi is the great self’ — and so on. Its meaning: the objects, the sense-objects, are higher than the indriyas — have become principal — because of their drawing them out. manas is higher than the objects, because the transaction of sense-object and indriya has manas as its root. Because the enjoyment of a sense-object is preceded by ascertainment, buddhi — whose nature is ascertainment — is higher than manas, whose nature is doubt. Because buddhi is an instrument of enjoyment, the enjoyer-self, the jīva, is higher than it. And that self is ‘the great one’ — the master of the body, the indriyas, and the inner instrument — that is its meaning. But bodily karma, by force of prior habituation, will proceed like the spinning of a wheel already set in motion.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.42",
      "textualNote": "The witness prints a bracketed modern editorial tag, ‘[gītā 2.59]’, after ‘raso 'py asya paraṃ dṛṣṭvā’, identifying the half-verse Baladeva cites; this is apparatus, not Baladeva's text, and is dropped here as the trailing ||NN|| markers are dropped elsewhere. The witness repeats the clause ‘viṣaya-bhogasya niścaya-pūrvakatvāt saṃśayātmakān manaso’ twice in immediate succession — an evident scribal dittography. Nothing is silently dropped: `sanskrit` above prints the repetition exactly as the witness has it, and `words[]` tokenizes both occurrences. Separately, ‘tad-ākarsiktatvāt’ does not parse as a standard derivative; it is read here as tad-ākarṣakatvāt (‘because of being what draws them’), the sense the passage plainly needs, but the witness's exact form is uncertain and flagged at the word.",
