      "sanskrit": "phalitam āha evam iti | raso 'py asya paraṃ dṛṣṭvā nivartate ity atra yaḥ para-śabdenoktas tam evambhūtaṃ pūrṇam ātmānaṃ buddheḥ paraṃ buddhvā sākṣātkṛtya saṃstabhya sthirīkṛtyātmānaṃ mana ātmanaitādṛśa-niścayātmikayā buddhyā jahi māraya śatruṃ sarva-puruṣārtha-śātanaṃ he mahābāho mahā-bāhor hi śatru-māraṇaṃ sukaram iti yogyaṃ sambodhanam | kāma-rūpaṃ tṛṣṇā-rūpaṃ durāsadaṃ duḥkhenāsādanīyaṃ durvijñeyāneka-viśeṣam iti yatnādhikyāya viśeṣaṇam",
      "ourRendering": "He states the upshot: ‘thus’ — thus. Here, in ‘relish too, the higher of this one having seen, it ceases’, he — who is denoted by the word ‘para’ — him, the self of such a nature, full, having known him beyond buddhi, having directly realized, having steadied, having made firm the self — the manas — by oneself, by such buddhi whose nature is this determination: ‘slay’, kill the enemy — the destroyer of every human goal. O ‘mighty-armed one’: since for the mighty-armed one the slaying of an enemy is easy — a fitting address. ‘Whose form is kāma’, whose form is tṛṣṇā, ‘hard to get at’ — to be reached with difficulty, whose many distinguishing forms are hard to know: for the sake of increased effort — a qualifying epithet.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.43",
