      "locus": "3.43",
      "sanskrit": "upasaṃharati evam iti | buddheḥ paraṃ jīvātmānaṃ buddhvā sarvopādhibhyaḥ pṛthak-bhūtaṃ jñātvā ātmanā svenaivātmānaṃ svaṃ saṃstabhya niścalaṃ kṛtvā durāsadaṃ durjayam api kāmaṃ jahi nāśaya | adhyāye 'smin sādhanasya niṣkāmasyaiva karmaṇaḥ | prādhānyam ūce tat-sādhya-jñānasya guṇatāṃ vadan || iti sārārtha-darśinyāṃ harṣiṇyāṃ bhakta-cetasām | tṛtīyaḥ khalu gītāsu saṅgataḥ saṅgataḥ satām ||",
      "ourRendering": "He concludes, in the word ‘evam’. The one beyond buddhi, the jīvātman, having known — distinct from all upādhis, having known it to have become — by one's own very self, one's own self, having steadied, having made unmoving — hard to get at, hard to conquer though it is, kāma: slay it, destroy it! In this chapter, the primacy of the sādhana — the karma without desire, alone — has been declared, while stating, of the knowledge to be accomplished by it, the status of a subordinate quality. Thus, in the Sārārtha-darśinī, the gladdener of the hearts of devotees, the third, among the Gītā's chapters, indeed, is coherent, coherent, for the good.",
      "source": "data/sources/sanskrit/vedanta/gretil_bhg4comm_full.txt (GRETIL: corpustei sa_bhagavadgItA-4comm), ad 3.43",
      "textualNote": "The on-disk witness prints ātmanā svenaivāmānaṃ svaṃ saṃstabhya, where āmānaṃ is not a Sanskrit word; the sense — ‘having steadied one's own self by one's own very self’, matching Śaṅkara's, Rāmānuja's and Madhva's parallel readings of this same clause — requires ātmānaṃ (a single dropped t). The reading given is ātmānaṃ. The closing colophon verse names the work sārārtha-darśinyām (‘in the Sārārtha-darśinī’); this reading is given exactly as the witness has it even though the metadata above follows the commoner modern citation Sārārtha-varṣiṇī — both names are attested for this commentary, and nothing here is altered to make them match.",
